    {
      "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10238671",
      "title": "\n            AlphaFold, allosteric, and orthosteric drug discovery: Ways forward - PMC\n        ",
      "publish_date": "2011-03-23",
      "excerpts": [
        "A massive hurdle facing AlphaFold in drug discovery is that proteins exist in ensembles of interconverting conformations, 61 whereas crystal structures and Alphafold’s models provide structural snapshots.\nAlternatively, one can apply molecular dynamics simulations to the AlphaFold model and use the resulting clustered conformations as input to docking methods. This appears to yield mixed performance. Simulations may or may not sample all relevant states.\nDel Alamo D, Sala D, McHaourab HS, Meiler J. Sampling alternative conformational states of transporters and receptors with AlphaFold2. Elife. 2022;11:e75751. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ]\n129. Ma P, Li DW, Brüschweiler R. Predicting protein flexibility with AlphaFold. Proteins. 2023. doi: 10.1002/prot.26471."
      ]
